    "q-141458": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141458",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nII. $T$ is a bijection\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ which means  $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
