    "q-141485": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141485",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T$ is not a bijection, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n\nIII. $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ In turn, there is $v\\ne 0$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
