    "q-38889": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38889",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Let $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
